    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ryse Loaded Protein is a solid 25 g whey blend wrapped in some of the most fun flavors on the shelf. It is not an isolate, it is not certified for tested sport, and it uses sucralose, but for a flavored daily whey at a mid-to-upper price it does the job.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse, sold under the company name RYSE Up Sports Nutrition, is a sports supplement brand that press coverage dates to 2017. It sells pre-workout, creatine, hydration mixes and energy drinks as well as protein, and its products are stocked at Target, Walmart, GNC, The Vitamin Shoppe, Dick's Sporting Goods, CVS and other chains, per the brand's own retailer list. What sets it apart is licensing: its protein comes in flavors built with brands such as Jet-Puffed, Skippy, Little Debbie, Kool-Aid and Country Time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flagship, Loaded Protein, delivers about 25 g of protein per scoop in roughly 130 to 150 calories depending on flavor, per retailer labels at the time of writing. The protein is a blend of whey isolate and whey concentrate, with added MCTs and prebiotic fiber, sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. That is a perfectly respectable spec for a flavored daily whey, roughly in line with what most big-brand blends offer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Ryse is weaker is transparency and verification. Its marketing leans on the word isolate even though concentrate is in the blend, and we could not find an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice listing for its protein. For most buyers that is a minor issue. For drug-tested athletes it rules the brand out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict: buy Ryse Loaded Protein if one of its flavors is the thing that will make you drink a shake every day and you are fine with sucralose. If you want the most protein for your money, or a certified product, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> ranking has better-value and better-verified options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A good flavored whey blend, not a premium isolate</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">About 25 g of protein from whey isolate and concentrate, 130 to 150 calories, sucralose-sweetened, with licensed dessert flavors. Worth buying for taste. Skip it for tested sport or if you want a pure isolate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ryse-or-ryze" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ryse or Ryze? Clearing Up the Name
          </h2>

          <p>
            A lot of people search for Ryze protein powder when they mean Ryse. The two names sound identical, but they are different companies. Ryze is a mushroom coffee brand known for its coffee alternative blends; it is not the company behind Loaded Protein. Ryse, spelled with an s, is the sports nutrition brand that makes the protein powders on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mix-up matters when you shop online. Searching the misspelling can surface mushroom coffee, unrelated products or lookalike listings. Check that the listing says RYSE, shows a Loaded Protein, Clear Protein or Loaded Plant Protein tub, and is sold by Amazon, the brand or a retailer you trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth being careful with copycat shopping sites. While researching this review, we came across several sites with lookalike domain names that copy Ryse product text and advertise steep discounts. Buy from the brand's own site, Amazon or a known retailer rather than an unfamiliar domain, and be suspicious of prices that look far below the usual range.
          </p>

          <p>
            With that out of the way, everything below refers to Ryse the supplement brand and its protein range: Loaded Protein, Clear Protein and, where you can still find it, Loaded Plant Protein.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Actually in Loaded Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Loaded Protein lists about 25 g of protein per scoop across flavors, per retailer labels. Retailers describe whey isolate as the primary source, and the ingredient list names both instantized whey protein isolate and instantized whey protein concentrate. That blend is normal for a flavored whey, and it is not a bad thing: concentrate costs less, tastes creamier and carries only a little more lactose and fat than isolate. It just is not a pure isolate, whatever the front of the tub implies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories and macros vary by flavor because the scoop weight changes. The Blueberry Muffin label shows about 140 calories, 2.5 g of fat and 4 g of carbohydrate in a scoop of about 35 g, while Skippy Peanut Butter shows about 150 calories, 3 g of fat and 5 g of carbohydrate in a scoop of about 40 g, with roughly 280 mg of sodium, per Vitacost listings. An HEB listing for a Little Debbie flavor shows about 130 calories. Expect 130 to 150 calories with 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryse adds MCTs and prebiotic fiber to the formula. The MCT dose is small enough that it mainly affects texture and creaminess rather than energy, and the prebiotic fiber is a minor extra. Neither is a reason to buy or avoid the product on its own, but together they explain why buyers often describe Loaded Protein as thicker and smoother than a bare whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners are sucralose and acesulfame potassium, per the ingredient list cited by Garage Gym Reviews. Both are FDA-approved and common in sports protein, but some people dislike their aftertaste or prefer to avoid them. If that is you, Ryse is not the right brand, and a stevia-sweetened powder such as those in our <a href="/clean-simple-eats-protein-powder-review/">Clean Simple Eats protein review</a> is a better direction.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
