        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Ryse Protein to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Loaded Protein is the one most people should buy. It is the flagship, it hits a full 25 g serving in one scoop, and it carries the licensed flavors that are the reason people choose Ryse in the first place. Buyers most often mention the Jet-Puffed Marshmallow, Skippy Peanut Butter and Little Debbie flavors, but taste is personal, so pick the flavor you would eat as a dessert.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear Protein is the pick if milky shakes feel heavy. It is described as 100% ultra-filtered whey isolate with about 22 g of protein and about 100 calories per serving and zero sugar, in flavors such as Kool-Aid Tropical Punch and Country Time Lemonade, per retailer listings. It mixes into a thin, juice-like drink. Ryse also sells a ready-to-drink Clear Whey in bottles if you want to skip mixing altogether. Our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide covers the wider category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loaded Plant Protein is the brand's vegan option. Retailer listings describe about 20 g of protein in a 30 g scoop from pea, chickpea and rice protein, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit, with added inulin fiber and MCTs. It does not appear on the brand's main protein page at the time of writing, so treat it as limited availability and check that a listing is current and in date before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tub sizes vary. Loaded Protein is sold in roughly 2 lb tubs of about 20 to 27 servings and larger tubs of about 4 lb, depending on flavor and retailer. The larger tub is almost always the better value per serving, but only if you are sure you like the flavor, so start small with a new one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse Loaded Protein is easy to calculate because one scoop is roughly one 25 g serving. Garage Gym Reviews put the cost at about $1.48 to $1.90 per serving depending on tub size, and Bodybuilding.com listed a 27-serving tub at about $50 at the time we checked, which is around $1.85 per serving. Call it roughly $1.50 to $1.90 per 25 g of protein, with sales pushing it lower. Prices change often, so treat these as approximate.
          </p>

          <p>
            That puts Ryse in the middle-to-upper part of the market. Large tubs of mainstream blends such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard usually cost noticeably less per 25 g at regular prices, while premium isolates and other licensed-flavor wheys such as Ghost tend to sit in a similar or higher range. You are paying a little extra for the flavors, not for a better protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear Protein costs more per gram, because clear isolates use a more processed protein and deliver about 22 g per serving. Expect it to come in above Loaded Protein per 25 g, and above it again if you buy ready-to-drink bottles. That premium is standard across clear proteins, not specific to Ryse.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest way to save is to buy the largest tub of a flavor you already know you like and watch for retailer promotions, which Ryse runs regularly through its big-box partners. If cost per 25 g is your main concern, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide covers the lowest-cost options.
