        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Certifications
          </h2>

          <p>
            We could not find an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification for Ryse protein at the time of writing, and the brand's main site makes no third-party testing claims that we could see. One retailer listing says the product is verified by an independent lab but does not name the lab. Without a named certifier or a published certificate, we treat Ryse as unverified.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean the product is unsafe or under-dosed. Many uncertified wheys are perfectly fine. It means you are relying on the brand's own quality control and the fact that it is made in the USA, which Bodybuilding.com's listing notes, rather than on an outside audit you can check yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            For drug-tested athletes the distinction is important. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Choice screen products for banned substances and check them on an ongoing basis, which is the only reliable protection against a positive test from a contaminated supplement. If you compete in a tested sport, pick a certified whey such as one of the Informed Choice options in our whey rankings and check the certifier database for your exact flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some consumer reviewers criticize Ryse for its artificial flavors and sweeteners. That is a preference rather than a safety issue: sucralose and acesulfame potassium are approved food additives. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide explains how to weigh testing claims, certifications and ingredient concerns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Ryse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Ryse if you avoid artificial sweeteners. Loaded Protein uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and the licensed flavors are sweet by design. Buyers who drink protein every day sometimes find that sweetness gets tiring, so if you prefer subtle flavors, a plainer whey or an unflavored powder you can flavor yourself is a better fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport, because we could not confirm an NSF or Informed Choice certification. Skip it too if you specifically need a pure isolate for lactose sensitivity. The concentrate in Loaded Protein adds a little lactose, and while many people tolerate it, those who react to small amounts should choose a straight isolate such as Dymatize ISO100 or the options in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Loaded Protein if you are vegan or have a milk allergy; it is dairy-based. Several flavors, including the Skippy ones, also involve peanut, so anyone with a peanut allergy should read the allergen statement on every flavor and avoid cross-contact risks.
          </p>

          <p>
            And as with any protein powder, if you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take medication or have a medical condition, check with a doctor or registered dietitian first. This review is a label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

