        <div id="use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Using Ryse Protein Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the flavors are so sweet, Ryse works best as a planned treat rather than as an all-day protein source. One scoop after training or as a dessert replacement gives you 25 g of protein in a format you will actually look forward to. For the rest of your daily target, lean on whole foods such as eggs, Greek yogurt, poultry and fish, which also bring micronutrients a shake does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix ratios change the experience a lot. Buyers often report that the dessert flavors taste richer with milk, but a cup of milk adds roughly 80 to 150 calories depending on fat content. If you are cutting, mix with water and ice and blend for a thicker texture without the calories. Clear Protein should always go with cold water, never milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some flavors, particularly the peanut butter and marshmallow ones, work well stirred into oats or Greek yogurt. As with any whey, avoid long, hot baking, which can make the protein dry and rubbery. Use it in no-bake recipes or add it to batters with plenty of moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, get your dose right. One scoop of Loaded Protein is a full 25 g serving for most people, which is enough to maximize the muscle-building response after a workout in most adults. Larger lifters may want closer to 40 g. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide shows how to set a daily total so the shake fills a real gap.
          </p>
        </div>

